{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And I, hiding, will hide.</b> It is difficult: why would He hide His face after they have confessed their sin and said ״הֲלֹא עַל כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי״? And if the verse gives a reason for what has transpired until now — that He hid His face from them — this should not need to be stated, for I see that the reason He hid is because they went astray after foreign gods, as He said above. Further, one must note in the verse why it doubled ״הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר.״ Further, one needs to know why He lengthened the expression to say ״עַל כָּל הָרָעָה״ and then returned to specify ״כִּי פָנָה.״" "<b>Rather,</b> the verse comes to give a reason for the hiding of the face. For one might say — heaven forbid — that HaShem did not judge with righteousness, heaven forbid, such a judgment, for the prophet said (Isaiah 27:8) ״בְּסַאסְּאָה בְּשַׁלְחָהּ״ etc., and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 8b): in the measure a person measures, it is measured for him — and He judges with righteousness, an El of faithfulness without iniquity." "<b>Now behold,</b> when the hiding of HaShem's face from them occurs, it could be that they are punished for their sin beyond the measure appropriate to them according to their punishment, for the Judge does not wish to know what will befall them from the troubles and evils — and this is not a just judgment, heaven forbid. Or perhaps this is included in the statement they make: ״הֲלֹא עַל כִּי אֵין אֱלֹהַי בְּקִרְבִּי מְצָאוּנִי״ etc." "<b>And the verse</b> comes to give a correct answer, and says ״וְאָנֹכִי הַסְתֵּר״ — meaning: this hiding, I have justice to hide My face from them. And therefore it doubled ״הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — meaning, on the day that they went astray after foreign gods. And it stated the reason: ״עַל כָּל הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר״ etc. — meaning, along the lines of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sifrei Re'eh 11:25; Chullin 5a): whoever acknowledges idolatry is as one who denies the entire Torah. And this is its saying that the reason for the hiding is ״עַל כָּל הָרָעָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה״ — and what is it? ״כִּי פָנָה אֶל אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים.״ From this point on, even if all the troubles in the world befall him on account of the hiding of the face, he deserves it, for just as he did all evil, so shall all troubles come upon him — and such is what the line of justice demands." "<b>And know</b> that there is no trouble and no evil in the world that does not correspond to a single transgression, for HaShem established sufferings corresponding to every sin, just as He fixed reward corresponding to every good deed. And since this is so, a person who transgressed the entire Torah, or idolatry which is equivalent to the entire Torah — the punishment is that all the punishments in the world come upon him. And therefore HaShem hid His face from them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:04.245167470Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}